<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- THE ENGINE INTERPRETER ONLY: `runtime/bin/python3.14`, signed by `build-bundled-engine`. Every
	     other binary in the engine is signed with no entitlements, and the build refuses one that
	     carries any.

	     allow-jit   docling-parse draws PDF pages with Blend2D, which JIT-compiles its raster
	                 pipelines into MAP_JIT memory. The hardened runtime refuses MAP_JIT without this
	                 key, and Blend2D does not fall back to drawing without the JIT. It returns the
	                 page buffer uninitialised instead. 0.3.0-0.3.2 shipped that: docling's layout model
	                 read noise, so every PDF converted with the models folder set came out as loose
	                 text blocks with no title and no table. Measured 2026-11-01 on docling-parse
	                 7.20.0 and 7.24.2.

	     WHAT IT WEAKENS: the engine process, which parses untrusted documents, may map memory that
	     flips between writable and executable. Memory corruption anywhere in that process has an
	     easier route to running code than it would under the full hardened runtime. Library
	     validation, the DYLD_* strip and the rest of the hardened runtime are unchanged.

	     NOT allow-unsigned-executable-memory, which also cures the render and permits ANY writable
	     and executable mapping, MAP_JIT or not. NOT disable-library-validation, which does not cure
	     it. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
